# Two synthetic spring-forward gaps, each standing in for a real zone whose
# rules are a moving target.
#
# Fixture/HalfHourGap — Australia/Lord_Howe's shape: a 30-minute gap, not an
# hour. 2026-10-04 02:00 local jumps to 02:30, so a skipped 02:15 must resolve
# to 02:45. This is what separates gap_delta's "read the transition's real
# width" from ActiveSupport's hardcoded `@time += 1.hour`, which overshoots to
# 03:15. Lord Howe has been stable for years, but it is the only zone in
# current use with a sub-hour gap — the coverage rests entirely on one real
# zone's continued existence, which this removes.
#
# Fixture/LateGap — America/Nuuk's shape: a gap late in the local day in a
# negative-offset zone, so the transition's UTC instant lands past the *next*
# UTC midnight (23:00 local at -02:00 is 01:00 UTC the following day). That is
# what forces gap_delta's scan window to be centered on the skipped wall clock
# read as UTC rather than on the UTC midnight of its date; a midnight-anchored
# window misses the transition entirely and gap_delta crashes. Nuuk only grew
# these rules in tzdata 2023a, and was named America/Godthab before 2020a, so
# a real-zone test for this edge is both vintage-dependent and, on older data,
# silently a different test.
Rule	Half	2000	max	-	Oct	Sun>=1	2:00	0:30	D
Rule	Half	2000	max	-	Apr	Sun>=1	2:00	0	S
Zone	Fixture/HalfHourGap	10:30	Half	%s
Rule	Late	2000	max	-	Mar	lastSat	23:00	1:00	D
Rule	Late	2000	max	-	Oct	lastSat	23:00	0	S
Zone	Fixture/LateGap	-2:00	Late	%s
